\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Unit split quaternions and compactness}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Unit quaternions give the compact group #{SU(2)}, as in
\ref{fdip-007G}. The split quaternions are the real algebra
#{\mathbb H'\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)}. Their norm-one elements give the
noncompact group #{SL(2,\mathbb R)}. Both groups are real forms of
#{SL(2,\mathbb C)}.}

\p{#{SL(2,\mathbb R)} has no finite Haar measure, so it has no
normalized Haar probability. The cited normalized Haar theorem
\ref{fdip-007B} uses compactness to fix total mass one. The character
expansions of \ref{fdip-007C} insert that probability into surface
integrals, and the continuum density of \ref{fdip-007F} uses the
compact real form so that #{\langle Y,Z\rangle=-\operatorname{tr}(YZ)}
is positive definite. Those three steps change on the split form: the
trace form becomes indefinite, and there is no probability to insert.
Compactness of the gauge group is a choice of real form. These notes
make no claim that the split form is physically wrong.}
